% OccSafe-VLA: Occupancy-Aware Safety for Vision-Language-Action Models
% Target: CoRL 2026
\documentclass{article}

\usepackage[dvipsnames]{xcolor}
\usepackage{corl_2026}

\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{enumitem}
\usepackage{subcaption}

\title{Does Auxiliary Spatial Supervision Make Vision-Language-Action Models Safer?\\ An Empirical Investigation}

\author{
Anonymous Author(s)
}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Fine-tuned Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models can achieve high task success yet remain behaviorally unsafe, with collision avoidance rates substantially lagging task completion on safety-critical benchmarks.
We present a systematic factorial study isolating how auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning affects collision avoidance, with twelve conditions varying gradient flow, label content, normalization type, spatial reference frame, and temporal extent.
Our central finding is negative: \textbf{all auxiliary spatial head configurations perform at or below the no-auxiliary-head baseline} on Collision Avoidance Rate. A $2{\times}2$ factorial (gradient flow $\times$ normalization) shows a pattern consistent with an ordinal interaction: gradient flow through the auxiliary head into the vision encoders is associated with lower safety ($\Delta{=}-4.2$\,pp within the BatchNorm conditions), and BatchNorm amplifies this deficit compared to LayerNorm. The interaction effect is large (partial $\eta^2{=}0.37$, Cohen's $d{=}1.41$) but the small-sample design ($n{\leq}3$ per cell) lacks power for formal confirmation. The BatchNorm + gradient flow cell is both the worst-performing and the most unstable configuration, with cross-seed standard deviation more than $2.5{\times}$ that of other cells.
Gradient analysis confirms orthogonality between action and auxiliary gradients (cosine similarity near zero), ruling out direct gradient conflict. Spatial label content (ground-truth occupancy, random binary labels, egocentric depth maps) does not affect safety outcomes, indicating that the gradient pathway, not the spatial information, determines the result.
Practical guidelines: do not add auxiliary spatial heads for safety purposes. If an auxiliary head is required by other objectives, use detached gradients with LayerNorm (Detach+LN), and never combine BatchNorm with gradient flow. The auxiliary head is discarded after training at zero inference cost.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Vision-Language-Action Models, Robot Safety, Auxiliary Supervision, Collision Avoidance}

\input{introduction}

\input{related_work}

\input{method}

\input{results}

\input{discussion}

\input{conclusion}

\bibliography{references}

\clearpage
\appendix
\input{appendix}

\end{document}

\section{Introduction}

Vision-language-action (VLA) models map visual observations and language instructions to robot actions, and recent fine-tuning recipes achieve high task success on standard manipulation benchmarks~\cite{brohan2023rt2, kim2024openvla, kim2025oft}. Behavioral safety, however, does not follow from task completion. On SafeLIBERO Level~I~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, OpenVLA-7B fine-tuned with the OFT recipe~\cite{kim2025oft} reaches a Task Success Rate (TSR) of 90.7\% but a Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) of only 78.0\%. On the three most constrained tasks, collision rates reach 43--93\%. These numbers expose a gap between what training objectives optimize for and what safe deployment requires: the robot completes the task but fails to avoid contact with surrounding objects. Identifying which fine-tuning design choices affect collision avoidance is a prerequisite for closing this gap.

Two active research threads address related but distinct aspects of this problem. The first enriches VLA models with auxiliary spatial prediction (occupancy grids, depth maps, point clouds) to inject 3D geometric structure into vision encoders~\cite{li2026spatialforcing, yuan2025depthvla, wu2025spatialvla, liu2025occvla}. At least fifteen such methods have appeared in the past year~\cite{wu2026stvla, chen2026gstvla, tu2026sgvla, xiao2026stemvla, li2025pointvla, sun2025geovla, bhat20253dcavla, li2025qdepthvla, abouzeid2025geoaware, rao2026augvla3d, li2026corridorvla}, all reporting improved task success. The implicit assumption in this literature is that better spatial awareness should also yield safer behavior, since the robot ``knows where things are.'' The second thread targets VLA safety directly through runtime action filtering~\cite{hu2025vlsa, zhai2026cofreevla, raja2024ogmcbf} or constrained reinforcement learning~\cite{zhang2025safevla}. These two lines of work are disconnected: no spatial method evaluates its effect on collision avoidance, and no safety method examines whether spatial representations contribute to or detract from safe behavior. A recent survey~\cite{li2026vlasafety} confirms that the relationship between auxiliary spatial supervision and collision safety remains an open question.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_overview.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Auxiliary spatial supervision does not improve VLA collision avoidance.} \textbf{(a)}~A fine-tuned OpenVLA-OFT achieves 90.7\% Task Success Rate but only 78.0\% Collision Avoidance Rate on SafeLIBERO Level~I; per-task collision rates reach 93\% on the most constrained tasks. \textbf{(b)}~Our factorial design varies five factors (gradient flow, label content, normalization type, spatial reference frame, temporal extent) across 12 conditions. An auxiliary spatial head is attached to the vision encoders during training and discarded at inference.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We ask a direct question: does auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning improve collision avoidance? We conduct a systematic factorial study using OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024openvla} with the OFT recipe~\cite{kim2025oft} and LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora} adaptation. Twelve experimental conditions vary five factors: gradient flow from the auxiliary head into the vision encoders, spatial label content, normalization type in the auxiliary head, spatial reference frame, and temporal extent of supervision (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}). This design is deliberate. Single-factor ablations, the dominant methodology in prior spatial VLA studies, cannot detect interactions between design choices. If gradient flow and normalization type interact in their effect on collision avoidance, a study that varies only one factor will attribute the joint effect to whichever factor it happens to test. Our $2{\times}2$ factorial subset (gradient flow $\times$ normalization) is the minimal design that can detect and quantify such interactions, and effect sizes suggest one that prior work could not have found.

The central result is negative: \textbf{all auxiliary spatial head configurations perform at or below the Vanilla baseline} on Collision Avoidance Rate. Auxiliary spatial supervision does not improve VLA collision avoidance under any of the twelve configurations tested. The $2{\times}2$ factorial subset (gradient flow $\times$ normalization) shows a pattern consistent with an ordinal interaction between gradient flow and normalization (partial $\eta^2{=}0.37$, Cohen's $d{=}1.41$), though the small-sample design ($n{\leq}3$ per cell) lacks power for formal confirmation. Gradient flow through the auxiliary head into the vision encoders is associated with lower safety ($\Delta{=}-4.2$\,pp within the BatchNorm conditions), and BatchNorm amplifies this deficit compared to LayerNorm. The BatchNorm + gradient flow cell (Full OCC) is both the worst-performing and the most unstable configuration, with cross-seed standard deviation of 0.064, more than 2.5 times that of the other factorial cells. Spatial label content does not affect outcomes: Full OCC (ground-truth occupancy), Random OCC (random binary labels), and Depth (egocentric depth maps) all fall within cross-seed variance of each other and of the Vanilla baseline. The spatial information itself, whether structured or random, is irrelevant to safety; the gradient pathway and normalization dynamics determine the outcome.

Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Negative core finding.} We show that auxiliary spatial supervision does not improve VLA collision avoidance under any of 12 configurations tested. A $2{\times}2$ factorial (gradient flow $\times$ normalization) shows effect sizes consistent with an ordinal interaction pattern (partial $\eta^2{=}0.37$): gradient flow is associated with lower safety, and BatchNorm amplifies the deficit and increases training instability. Spatial label content (structured, random, depth) does not affect outcomes (\S\ref{sec:results}, Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, Figure~\ref{fig:factorial}).

    \item \textbf{Factorial methodology for VLA auxiliary heads.} We introduce the first systematic factorial comparison of how auxiliary head design choices interact during VLA fine-tuning. This design can detect interactions that single-factor ablations would miss (\S\ref{sec:ablation}, Table~\ref{tab:ablation_design}).

    \item \textbf{Actionable guidelines at zero cost.} Do not add auxiliary spatial heads for the purpose of improving collision avoidance. If an auxiliary head is required by other objectives, use detached gradients with LayerNorm (Detach+LN), and never combine BatchNorm with gradient flow. No ground-truth spatial labels are needed; the auxiliary head is discarded at inference (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Related Work}

\subsection{VLA Safety and Spatial Representations}

Vision-language-action models achieve high task success rates but remain prone to collisions in cluttered environments~\cite{li2026vlasafety}. Existing safety methods fall into two categories. \emph{Runtime constraint methods} add safety layers at inference time: AEGIS~\cite{hu2025vlsa} constructs a control barrier function (CBF) from vision-language obstacle detection and filters unsafe actions, improving the collision avoidance rate by 59\,pp on SafeLIBERO; CoFreeVLA~\cite{zhai2026cofreevla} trains a collision risk estimator for dual-arm coordination; OGM-CBF~\cite{raja2024ogmcbf} derives CBFs from occupancy grid maps. \emph{Training-time methods} embed safety into the policy directly: SafeVLA~\cite{zhang2025safevla} applies constrained reinforcement learning to reduce safety violations by 84\% (NeurIPS 2025 Spotlight). A recent survey~\cite{li2026vlasafety} catalogues both approaches and confirms that the relationship between spatial representations and collision safety remains unstudied.

A separate and rapidly growing line of work enriches VLA models with 3D geometric structure to boost task success. Depth-based methods fuse monocular depth predictions into visual features~\cite{yuan2025depthvla,li2025qdepthvla,rao2026augvla3d,bhat20253dcavla,abouzeid2025geoaware}, typically leveraging foundation depth models such as Depth Anything~\cite{yang2024depthanything}. Alignment-based approaches regularize the VLA's internal representations toward 3D consistency: Spatial Forcing~\cite{li2026spatialforcing} aligns VLA embeddings with pretrained 3D features via an auxiliary loss (ICLR 2026), while SpatialVLA~\cite{wu2025spatialvla} introduces an egocentric 3D position encoding (RSS 2025). Point-cloud and geometric methods encode explicit 3D structure through various representations~\cite{li2025pointvla,sun2025geovla,wu2026stvla,chen2026gstvla,tu2026sgvla,xiao2026stemvla,li2026corridorvla}. In autonomous driving, OccVLA~\cite{liu2025occvla} uses voxel occupancy prediction as auxiliary VLA supervision, drawing on BEV and occupancy representations from perception~\cite{li2022bevformer,sima2023occnet,tian2023occ3d}. All of these spatial methods report improved task success, yet \emph{none} evaluates their effect on collision avoidance.

Our work addresses this gap: we study whether auxiliary spatial supervision affects safety, and find that it does not improve collision avoidance under any configuration tested.

\subsection{Auxiliary Tasks and Multi-Task Gradient Dynamics}

Multi-task learning exploits shared representations across related objectives to improve generalization~\cite{caruana1997multitask,ruder2017overview}. In overparameterized networks, multi-task training induces implicit $\ell_{1,2}$ regularization, whereas single-task fine-tuning exhibits nested feature selection that discards unused pretrained features~\cite{lippl2024implicit}. A parallel line of work addresses gradient conflicts that arise when task gradients oppose each other, proposing surgery and balancing strategies~\cite{chen2018gradnorm,yu2020pcgrad,liu2021cagrad,navon2022nashmtl}. These methods share a common premise: conflicting gradients must be reconciled for effective joint optimization. Intermediate supervision at hidden layers~\cite{lee2015deeply} offers an alternative perspective: auxiliary losses inject gradient signal into early layers, shaping feature learning independently of gradient conflict. Gradient starvation~\cite{pezeshki2021gradient} further shows that removing a gradient source can cause certain feature directions to receive vanishingly small updates, highlighting the sensitivity of representation learning to the set of active losses. ForkMerge~\cite{jiang2023forkmerge} decomposes negative transfer in auxiliary-task learning into optimization conflict and generalization gap, showing that resolving gradient conflict alone does not eliminate negative transfer; our finding that auxiliary spatial gradients remain orthogonal to action gradients yet gradient flow still degrades safety is consistent with this generalization-side failure mode.

Batch normalization (BN) statistics are known to encode distributional assumptions that, when violated, degrade performance. Li et al.~\cite{li2017adabn} show that adapting BN running statistics alone achieves effective domain adaptation, and Schneider et al.~\cite{schneider2020bn} demonstrate that replacing BN statistics with test-time estimates substantially improves robustness to input corruptions. These findings establish that BN layers act as implicit distribution descriptors, making them a sensitive channel through which auxiliary task gradients can affect the shared representation.

For VLA fine-tuning with LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora}, the interaction between auxiliary losses and low-rank adapter updates has not been studied. Among spatial VLA methods, Spatial Forcing~\cite{li2026spatialforcing} uses an auxiliary depth-alignment loss and reports faster convergence, but does not disentangle the regularization effect of auxiliary gradient flow from the informational content of the spatial signal. Our factorial design isolates these factors and identifies a pattern consistent with an ordinal interaction: gradient flow is associated with lower safety regardless of normalization choice, and BatchNorm amplifies the deficit---a finding that challenges the implicit assumption that auxiliary spatial supervision should improve collision avoidance.

