\subsection{Normalization--Gradient Flow Interaction}
\label{sec:mechanism}

The four conditions crossing normalization type (BN/LN) with gradient flow (ON/OFF) form a $2{\times}2$ factorial (Table~\ref{tab:factorial}, Figure~\ref{fig:factorial}). Within the fully v2 BN cells, gradient flow degrades CAR by $-$4.2\,pp (Full OCC 0.725 vs.\ Detach OCC 0.767; Hedges' $g{=}-0.781$, bootstrap 95\% CI $[-0.108, 0.014]$). The LN cells show the same directional pattern ($-$2.5\,pp), yielding a pattern consistent with an ordinal interaction (partial $\eta^2{=}0.37$, Cohen's $d{=}1.41$; formal confirmation requires larger $n$): gradient flow is associated with lower safety regardless of normalization, and BatchNorm amplifies the deficit. We report effect sizes and confidence intervals for transparency; the small-$n$ factorial design prioritizes breadth across a 12-condition space over statistical power for individual pairwise tests. The directional consistency---all twelve conditions perform at or below Vanilla---is itself informative: under a null hypothesis that auxiliary supervision has a positive effect on safety, observing zero improvements in twelve distinct configurations has $p < 0.001$ (binomial test, assuming independence; some structural dependence exists among conditions sharing architectural components).

\textbf{Ordinal, not additive.} Both BN and LN lines slope downward from Gradient OFF to Gradient ON; the lines do not cross (Figure~\ref{fig:factorial}). With BN, gradient flow degrades CAR by $-$4.2\,pp (Full OCC 0.725 vs.\ Detach 0.767). With LN, gradient flow degrades CAR by $-$2.5\,pp (Full OCC+LN 0.740 vs.\ Detach+LN 0.765). The larger BN deficit suggests that BatchNorm statistics amplify the detrimental effect of auxiliary gradient flow on safety behavior.

\textbf{Full OCC (BN) is the worst and most unstable configuration.} Full OCC with BatchNorm and gradient flow (CAR 0.725) falls 4.3\,pp below Vanilla, with cross-seed standard deviation of 0.064---more than 2.5 times that of the other factorial cells ($\sigma \leq 0.024$). Individual seeds range from 0.654 to 0.776, indicating that the BN + gradient flow combination creates a volatile optimization landscape.

