\subsection{Gradient Dynamics}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/gradient_analysis_combined.pdf}
\caption{Gradient dynamics during VLA fine-tuning (preliminary analysis using v1 codebase checkpoint; seed 42, 200 batches at 50K checkpoint). (a)~Action loss gradient norms per encoder layer: Vanilla, Full OCC, and Random OCC are comparable, while Detach shows substantially reduced norms. (b)~Auxiliary loss gradient norms in the shared encoder (Detach excluded; no auxiliary gradients by design). (c)~Action--auxiliary gradient cosine similarity per layer (near zero for both Full OCC and Random OCC, which confirms orthogonality). (d)~Gradient norm ratio (Detach / Vanilla) per layer. V2 validation is ongoing; directional conclusions should be interpreted with caution.}
\label{fig:gradient_analysis}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_analysis} examines gradient dynamics across conditions. All gradient norms are computed with respect to the L1 action loss (action tokens only), not the full language modeling objective.

\textbf{Gradient orthogonality.} Action--auxiliary gradient cosine similarity is near zero across all encoder layers for both Full OCC and Random OCC (Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_analysis}c), with conflict rates of ${\sim}$50\%. This confirms that action and auxiliary gradients are orthogonal: direct gradient conflict is not the mechanism through which auxiliary heads affect safety. Action loss gradient norms are comparable across Vanilla and gradient-flow conditions, which rules out gradient amplification. Both patterns are identical for structured and random labels, consistent with the behavioral finding that spatial content does not affect outcomes.

\textbf{Mechanism remains open.} The observed interaction pattern---gradient flow is associated with lower safety, BatchNorm amplifies the deficit---cannot be explained by gradient conflict (orthogonality rules it out) or by gradient amplification (norms are comparable). The detrimental effect likely operates through indirect modification of the LoRA optimization trajectory under a dual-objective loss landscape, but the precise mechanism requires further investigation.

\textbf{Representational structure is preserved across all conditions.} CKA between fine-tuned and pretrained encoder features shows that all conditions, including the worst-performing Full OCC (BN), maintain high similarity to the pretrained representations (CKA $\geq$ 0.95 for both DINOv2 and SigLIP encoders). The safety degradation under gradient flow therefore does not stem from representational corruption: the encoders retain their pretrained structure, yet the policy behaves less safely. This dissociation between representational fidelity and behavioral safety reinforces the conclusion that the detrimental effect operates through optimization dynamics rather than representation distortion.

Additional observations (per-task breakdown, depth comparison, and temporal dynamics) are in Appendix~\ref{app:additional}.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:why_gradient}

The observed interaction pattern indicates that BatchNorm is not inherently harmful: Detach OCC (BN, gradient OFF) achieves CAR 0.767, indistinguishable from Vanilla (0.768, $d{=}-0.04$). The damage emerges specifically when auxiliary gradients flow through the shared encoders. Cell~1 (BN + gradient ON) exhibits $\sigma{=}0.064$---more than $2.5{\times}$ that of the architecturally identical Cell~3 (BN + gradient OFF, $\sigma{=}0.024$)---with individual seeds ranging from near-Vanilla (0.776) to catastrophic ($\Delta{=}-11.4$\,pp). This variance asymmetry, driven solely by the presence of auxiliary gradient flow, indicates that the interaction between auxiliary optimization and BatchNorm statistics introduces unpredictable safety degradation. Under gradient flow, the encoder features are reshaped to serve dual objectives; BatchNorm's running statistics, computed over the joint activation distribution, absorb this distributional shift and propagate it to downstream action decoding. LayerNorm, which normalizes per-token independently of the batch, dampens this propagation---consistent with the smaller LN deficit ($-$2.5\,pp vs.\ $-$4.2\,pp under BN). The damage is independent of spatial content: Full OCC, Random OCC, and Depth all achieve comparable CAR, confirming that the gradient pathway, not the spatial information, determines safety outcomes.

Gradient analysis confirms orthogonality between action and auxiliary gradients (cosine $\approx$ 0, conflict rate $\approx$ 50\%), ruling out direct gradient conflict~\cite{yu2020pcgrad, liu2021cagrad}. The detrimental effect operates through indirect channels---likely reshaping the LoRA optimization trajectory under a dual-objective loss landscape~\cite{lippl2024implicit, jiang2023forkmerge}---rather than through gradient interference. This sensitivity extends to implementation details: a codebase revision affecting only the auxiliary head pipeline (identical hyperparameters) shifts absolute CAR by up to 8.6\,pp for the same seed, while the Vanilla baseline remains stable.

These findings carry implications for the spatial VLA literature, where at least fifteen methods add auxiliary spatial prediction during fine-tuning~\cite{li2026spatialforcing, yuan2025depthvla, wu2025spatialvla, liu2025occvla}, all reporting improved task success without evaluating collision avoidance. Our results show that auxiliary gradient flow can degrade safety even when task success is maintained, and that the degradation is sensitive to normalization type and implementation choices that are rarely reported. The disconnect between task success and collision avoidance means that spatial VLA methods must report safety metrics alongside task success; normalization type and gradient flow configuration---rarely specified in current work---should be reported as standard practice, given that these choices alone can shift collision avoidance by up to 4\,pp.

\textbf{Why spatial supervision can improve task success without improving safety.} The disconnect between TSR and CAR has a structural explanation. On SafeLIBERO Level~I, 7 of 10 tasks already exhibit CAR $\geq$ 0.92 under the Vanilla baseline, leaving little room for spatial supervision to improve safety on these tasks (ceiling effect). The remaining 3 tasks with high collision rates (43--93\%) involve tight spatial constraints where collisions arise from physical proximity between the manipulation target and obstacles, not from a lack of spatial awareness. Auxiliary spatial prediction can help the policy locate objects (benefiting task completion) without teaching it to avoid contact in geometrically constrained configurations. Spatial supervision thus addresses a different bottleneck than the one that drives collision failures.

\textbf{Statistical power.} The $2{\times}2$ factorial design prioritizes breadth (12 conditions, 5 factors) over per-cell statistical power. For the BN row, the observed effect size is large (Hedges' $g{=}-0.781$), but with $n{=}3$ vs.\ $n{=}4$ seeds per cell, post-hoc power is approximately 0.3--0.4~\cite{cohen1988statistical}, meaning there is a 60--70\% probability of failing to detect a true effect of this magnitude at $\alpha{=}0.05$. We report this limitation transparently. However, the study's evidential strength does not rest on any single pairwise comparison. The directional consistency across all twelve conditions---every configuration performs at or below Vanilla---yields $p < 0.001$ under a binomial null (probability 0.5 of improvement), providing strong global evidence that auxiliary spatial supervision does not improve collision avoidance. Seed expansion ($n{=}5$ per factorial cell) is underway to increase per-cell power.

\textbf{Practical guideline.} Do not add auxiliary spatial heads for the purpose of improving collision avoidance; all tested configurations perform at or below the Vanilla baseline. If an auxiliary head is required by other objectives, use detached gradients with LayerNorm (Detach+LN), and never combine BatchNorm with gradient flow. No ground-truth spatial labels are needed; the head is discarded at zero inference cost, complementing runtime safety methods~\cite{hu2025vlsa, raja2024ogmcbf}. Limitations are discussed in Appendix~\ref{app:limitations}.

\section{Conclusion}

We presented a systematic factorial study asking whether auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning improves collision avoidance. The answer is no: all twelve configurations perform at or below the no-auxiliary-head baseline. Effect sizes from a $2{\times}2$ factorial (gradient flow $\times$ normalization) identify the conditions under which auxiliary tasks hurt: gradient flow through the auxiliary head is associated with lower safety ($\Delta{=}-4.2$\,pp within BatchNorm conditions, Cohen's $d{=}1.41$), BatchNorm amplifies this deficit and increases training instability ($\sigma$ more than $2.5{\times}$ other cells), and spatial label content is irrelevant to the outcome. The precise mechanism remains open---gradient orthogonality rules out direct conflict, pointing instead to indirect effects on the LoRA optimization trajectory---but the behavioral pattern is robust across all conditions tested. Guidelines: do not add auxiliary spatial heads for safety; if required by other objectives, use Detach+LN and never combine BatchNorm with gradient flow (no ground-truth labels needed, zero inference cost).

CKA analysis confirms that encoder representations remain intact under all conditions (CKA $\geq$ 0.95), localizing the safety degradation to the optimization trajectory rather than representational capacity. Because the problem lies in how auxiliary gradients reshape LoRA updates rather than in what spatial information the labels encode, training schedule design (as suggested by our cutoff experiments) may be a more promising lever than label content for safety-relevant outcomes. More broadly, training-time collision avoidance may require approaches that directly encode safety constraints---such as constrained optimization~\cite{zhang2025safevla} or safety-aware reward shaping---rather than auxiliary prediction tasks that improve spatial awareness without shaping the policy's collision behavior.

